<worksheet xml:id="WP-10">
    <title>Week 10 Weekly Practice</title>
   <page>

    <p>
        Due the first class of the week of November 9.
    </p>

   

    <exercise>
  <title>Exercise 5.2. (f)</title>

<introduction>
   <p>You are investigating a muuuuurderr. The following table lists the possibilities.</p>

<table>
  <title>Who, What, When, Where</title>
  <tabular halign="center">
    <row header="yes" bottom="minor" >
      <cell>Killer </cell>
      <cell>Murder Weapon </cell>
      <cell>Time of Crime </cell>
      <cell>Location </cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>Miss Scarlet (S)</cell>
      <cell>Wrench (W)</cell>
      <cell>Noon (D)</cell>
      <cell>Billiard Room (B)</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>Colonel Mustard (M)</cell>
      <cell>Knife (Kn)</cell>
      <cell>Midnight (N)</cell>
      <cell>Kitchen (K)</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>Professor Plum (P)</cell>
      <cell>Candlestick (C)</cell>
      <cell></cell>
      <cell>Library (L)</cell>
    </row>

  </tabular>

</table>


<p>
  You have the following additional facts that have been 
  established at the scene of the crime.
</p>
<p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <p>
        Either the weapon was the wrench or the crime took place in the billiard room (but not both)

      </p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <p>
        If the crime took place at midnight, then Colonel Mustard is guilty.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Professor Plum is innocent if and only if the weapon was not the wrench.
      </p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <p>
        If the weapon was the candlestick, then Miss Scarlet is guilty.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        If the crime occurred at noon, then it was not in the library and the weapon was not a knife.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</p>

<p>
  Now, as a side hustle, you are also a mathematician. As such, you start wondering 
  what further piece of evidence would conclusively determine the killer. 
  For each of the following, explain how that piece of evidence, if established,  
  would determine the killer. </p>

</introduction>
<task workspace = "2in">
  <statement>
    <p>
      The weapon was the wrench.
    </p>
  </statement>
</task>
  <task workspace = "2in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The crime took place in the billiard room.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>


</exercise>
</page>


<page>
<exercise>
  <title>Exercise 5.3</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Determine whether the following are statements. 
      Among those that are, determine whether it is true or false.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <task workspace = ".5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        2+3=5

      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
  <task workspace = ".5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The sets <m>\mathbb{Z}</m> and <m>\mathbb{Q}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>

  <task workspace = ".5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The sets <m>\mathbb{Z}</m> and <m>\mathbb{Q}</m> both contain 2.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>

  <task workspace = ".5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        8675309 is a prime number
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>

  <task workspace = ".5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Every integer is a real number.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>

  <task workspace = ".5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Every real number is an integer.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
</exercise>

</page>
<page>
<exercise>
  <title>Exercise 5.22</title>
  
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Construct truth tables to 
      prove the following logical equivalences, 
      for open sentences <m>P,Q</m> and <m>R.</m>
    </p>
  </introduction>
<task workspace = "3in">
  <p>
     <m>\sim (P\vee Q) \equiv \sim P \wedge \sim Q</m>
  </p>
</task>
<task workspace = "3in">
  <statement>
    <p>
      <m>P\implies (Q\vee R) \equiv (P\implies Q) \wedge (P\implies R) </m>
    </p>
  </statement>
</task>


</exercise>

</page>
<page>
<exercise>
  <title>Exercise 5.10</title>
  
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Negate the following sentences. You do not need to determine the truth value!
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <task workspace = ".5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        For every prime <m>p</m>, there exists a prime <m>q</m> for which <m>q &gt; p</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>

  <task workspace = ".5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Every polynomial is differentiable.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>

  <task workspace = ".5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        For all real numbers <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>, if <m>xy=0</m>, then <m>x=0</m> or <m>y=0</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
  <task workspace = ".5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        If <m>mn</m> is odd, then <m>m</m> is odd, and <m>n</m> is odd.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>

  <task workspace = ".5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        For all natural numbers <m>p</m> if <m>p</m> is prime then 
        <m>\sqrt{p} \notin \mathbb{Q}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
  <task workspace = ".5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        If I pass Algebra I and Analysis I this semester, then I will take Algebra II or Analysis II next semester.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>

  <task workspace = ".5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        For every <m>\epsilon &gt; 0</m> there exists an <m>N</m> such that 
        <m>N&gt; n</m> implies <m>|an-a|&lt;\epsilon</m>. 
        (Note <m>\epsilon</m> is the Greek letter <q>epsilon</q>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
</exercise>
</page>


